insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-proof-test-interval', 1, 'What does `proofTestSensitivity` hold still, and what does it move, when it builds a table of PFDavg against the proof test interval?', '["It moves T1 and re-fits the beta factor at each interval so the common cause share stays constant.", "It moves T1 and lengthens the MTTR alongside it, since a site that tests less often also repairs more slowly.", "It holds every other input still and moves only T1, so what comes back is the PFDavg the same subsystem achieves at each interval in the list with nothing else changed between the rows.", "It searches for the one interval that just meets a stated target PFDavg and reports it with a state."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine takes one subsystem and a list of intervals in hours, holds every other input and returns the PFDavg at each one, which is what makes the columns comparable. The beta factor and the MTTR are typed inputs and the sweep never edits them. Searching against a target is the work of `maxProofTestInterval`, which returns an interval and a state.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-proof-test-interval', 2, 'The EKULAMA channel as a 1oo1 with undetected failures only reads 0.005256000000 at 8760 hours. What does the same channel read at 17520 hours?', '["0.021024000000, which is the figure the sensitivity sweep prints against 35040 hours", "0.002628000000, which is the figure the sensitivity sweep prints against 4380 hours", "0.005256000000, because a channel carrying no detected failures is unmoved by T1", "0.010512000000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The sweep prints 0.010512000000 at 17520 hours. 0.021024000000 belongs to 35040 hours and 0.002628000000 to 4380 hours, so both are read off the wrong row. Repeating 0.005256000000 denies the sweep, since this channel is linear in T1 and its printed ratio is 2.000000 at every doubling.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-proof-test-interval', 3, 'In the same sweep the IDU valves as a 1oo2 read 0.000611621000 at 4380 hours. What do they read at 8760 hours?', '["0.000300179812, which is the figure printed for the valves against 2190 hours", "0.002847929478, which is the figure printed for the valves against 17520 hours", "0.006810104389, which is the figure printed for the valves against 35040 hours", "0.001287026426"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine returns 0.001287026426 for the IDU valves on a one year test. 0.000300179812, 0.002847929478 and 0.006810104389 are the 2190, 17520 and 35040 hour rows of the same column, each the right arithmetic at the wrong interval.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-proof-test-interval', 4, 'Down the whole ratio column for the EKULAMA 1oo1 with undetected failures only, every doubling prints the same figure. Which figure, and why?', '["2.049485, because the MRT enters the down time once and makes the growth of this column slightly faster than linear at every step of the sweep.", "2.000000, because a 1oo1 with no detected failures is linear in T1, so each doubling of the interval doubles the PFDavg.", "4.000000, because the failure rate and the interval both enter the form, so their product grows with the square.", "2.104288, because the common cause term grows linearly and the independent term with the square."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The printed column reads 2.000000 at every doubling. 2.049485 and 2.104288 are the first doublings of the EKULAMA full 1oo2 and of the IDU valves, which are redundant and carry two kinds of term. 4.000000 is the limit the redundant columns climb toward without reaching it in this sweep.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-proof-test-interval', 5, 'Why do the three redundant columns of the ratio table start just above two and climb as the interval grows?', '["The bisection that produced the table converges more slowly at long intervals, so the later rows carry a numerical error that accumulates steadily down the column.", "The common cause term is linear in T1 and dominates at one year, and as T1 grows the independent term, which grows with the square of T1, takes a larger share.", "The beta factor is applied afresh at each interval, and a longer interval leaves a larger fraction common.", "The MTTR is charged once per interval, so a longer interval charges it fewer times."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The digest gives the mechanism: these subsystems are common cause dominated at one year, the common cause term is linear in T1, and the independent term grows with the square of T1 and takes a larger share as T1 grows, so the ratio climbs toward four without reaching it here. The sweep is not produced by bisection, the beta factor is a held input, and the MTTR is not recharged per interval.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-proof-test-interval', 6, 'The IDU valves are SIL 3 at 2190 hours and SIL 2 at 8760 hours. Which band does the engine report for them at 70080 hours?', '["SIL 2, which is the band the valves also hold at 17520 and at 35040 hours", "SIL 3, because a redundant final element keeps its band right across the sweep", "No band at all, because the achieved figure has left the low demand table", "SIL 1"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The digest lists the band for every row of the valve column, and 70080 hours is SIL 1. The valves hold SIL 2 at 8760, 17520 and 35040 hours, so stopping at SIL 2 stops one row short, and SIL 3 holds only at 2190 and 4380 hours. A figure of 0.018095929431 sits inside the table, so no band at all is wrong too.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-proof-test-interval', 7, 'This course legislates how a credited protection layer''s figure and a subsystem''s averaged figure are each written. Which pair is correct?', '["IPL PFD for a credited protection layer, and PFDavg for a subsystem or for a whole safety instrumented function, which is an average over the proof test interval.", "PFDavg for both, since one average serves a credited layer and a function alike.", "The risk reduction factor for a credited layer and PFDavg for a function.", "IPL PFDavg for a credited layer and PFDavg for a subsystem, differing only in the prefix."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The vocabulary this course legislates writes a credited layer''s figure as an IPL PFD and a subsystem''s or a function''s figure as PFDavg. A credited layer''s figure is a single probability and is never an average over an interval, so neither PFDavg nor IPL PFDavg fits it. The risk reduction factor is one over PFDavg and is a different quantity again.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-proof-test-interval', 8, 'How does `maxProofTestInterval` find the longest interval that meets a target, and what makes its answer unique?', '["It inverts the Annex B form for the architecture in hand, which gives a closed expression for T1 and therefore exactly one root by construction, whatever the inputs.", "It steps T1 upward from the shortest interval in one hour increments and stops at the first interval whose PFDavg has passed the target.", "Bisection on T1 of the Annex B PFDavg, which is non-decreasing in T1, so there is exactly one crossing.", "It runs the six interval sweep and reports the last of the six still under the target."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine''s method, in its own words, is bisection on T1 of the Annex B PFDavg, which is non-decreasing in T1, and it converges to one part in a trillion. Bisection needs only that the function can be evaluated, so no form is inverted for any architecture. A one hour step and a six row sweep would both return a coarse interval, while the engine returns 12957.474301 hours against a target of 0.002.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-proof-test-interval', 9, 'Against a target PFDavg of 0.002, for how many hours may the IDU valves go between proof tests?', '["6953.661742 hours, which is the answer the engine gives against a tighter target of 0.001", "12957.474301 hours", "27640.443736 hours, which is the answer against a looser target of 0.005", "3609.141022 hours"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The valve row against a target of 0.002 gives 12957.474301 hours, 1.479164 years, with the state FOUND. 6953.661742 hours belongs to a target of 0.001, 27640.443736 hours to 0.005 and 3609.141022 hours to 0.0005, so each wrong figure is the right search against the wrong target.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-proof-test-interval', 10, 'Every FOUND row of the longest interval table carries a final column giving the PFDavg achieved at the interval returned. What does that column show, and what is it for?', '["It shows the target itself, which is how a reader sees by measurement that the search stopped exactly at the crossing.", "It shows the PFDavg at half the returned interval, the last value the bisection tried.", "It shows the floor this subsystem cannot reach below whatever interval is chosen.", "It shows the PFDavg at the lifetime, which bounds the interval from above."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Against a target of 0.0005 the engine returns 3609.141022 hours and the column reads 0.000500000000, which is the target, so the column is the check on the search. A floor belongs to a partially tested subsystem and is reported on an UNACHIEVABLE row, and the lifetime bound belongs to a CAPPED_AT_LIFETIME row.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-proof-test-interval', 11, 'A 1oo1 with an undetected rate of 1e-6 per hour, a detected rate of 2e-4 per hour and an MTTR of 72 hours is searched against a target of 0.01, and the engine returns UNACHIEVABLE. What figure sits beside that state?', '["A floor of 0.000008000000, because the detected failures leave a residue sitting above the target whatever interval is chosen for the proof test on this channel.", "A PFDavg of 0.010000000000, because the engine echoes the target back when no interval can be found and leaves the interval field empty.", "A floor of 0.014400000000, because the part of the PFDavg that does not depend on T1 already reaches the target.", "No figure at all, because a state carrying no interval carries no number either."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The digest prints a floor of 0.014400000000 for this channel, above the target of 0.01, so no interval however short meets it. 0.000008000000 is what the INTERVAL_INDEPENDENT channel reports. The engine never echoes a target back, and the state does carry the floor, which is what makes it a design finding a note can quote.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-proof-test-interval', 12, 'A longest interval search comes back with the state INTERVAL_INDEPENDENT. What has the engine found, and what does it report?', '["That the target is met at every interval the engine tried before the bisection gave up, and it reports the last value it evaluated on the way.", "That the call was declined because an undetected rate of zero leaves no dangerous failure to average, so it reports a null in place of a figure.", "That the answer is set by the detected failures alone and therefore sits at a floor, which the engine reports as 0.014400000000.", "That the subsystem has no undetected failures, so T1 never enters the arithmetic, and it reports 0.000008000000."]'::jsonb, 3, 'With no undetected failures T1 does not enter the arithmetic at all, so the reported 0.000008000000 is what that channel achieves at every interval. The engine returns a figure rather than a null, and on the rates it refuses only when the undetected and detected rates are BOTH zero. The floor of 0.014400000000 belongs to the UNACHIEVABLE channel.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-proof-test-interval', 13, 'A 1oo1 with an undetected rate of 2e-8 per hour, no detected failures, a coverage of 0.9 and a lifetime of 87600 hours is searched against a target of 0.01. What comes back?', '["No interval and the state UNACHIEVABLE, because a coverage below one puts a floor under the answer that a target of this size can never reach at all.", "87600.000000 hours with the state CAPPED_AT_LIFETIME, and a PFDavg of 0.000876000000 at that interval.", "An unbounded interval, since the target is met at every interval tried.", "46473.426980 hours with the state FOUND, a crossing well inside the lifetime."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The target is met even at the lifetime, so the engine returns the lifetime of 87600.000000 hours with the state CAPPED_AT_LIFETIME, and 0.000876000000 is achieved there. No interval longer than the lifetime is meaningful, so neither an unbounded interval nor UNACHIEVABLE is right. 46473.426980 hours is the IDU valve answer against a target of 0.01.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-proof-test-interval', 14, 'A sensitivity call arrives with an empty list of intervals. What does the engine do?', '["It declines the call and names the field, in its own words: intervalsHours: must be a non-empty list of proof test intervals in hours", "It returns an empty table with the subsystem''s inputs echoed back.", "It substitutes the six teaching intervals and returns that table.", "It returns one row, at the subsystem''s own proof test interval."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The message quoted above is the engine''s own, and it names the field `intervalsHours`. A refusal replaces the result entirely and carries no number, so nothing is echoed back and no row is returned. The engine invents no input, which rules out any substituted sweep.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-proof-test-interval', 15, 'A longest interval search is run with a target PFDavg of one. What comes back?', '["The state CAPPED_AT_LIFETIME, since every subsystem meets a target of one and the lifetime is therefore the honest bound for the engine to report back.", "The state INTERVAL_INDEPENDENT, since a target every interval satisfies makes the answer independent of the interval in just the sense that state names.", "A refusal naming `targetPfdAvg`, in the engine''s words: targetPfdAvg: must lie in (0, 1)", "A result carrying the lifetime and a warning that the target was loose."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A target of one has no crossing to find, and the engine declines with the message above. That is a finding about the CALL. UNACHIEVABLE, INTERVAL_INDEPENDENT and CAPPED_AT_LIFETIME are findings about the DESIGN, where the call succeeded and the honest result is that no interval meets the target or that the interval does not enter. No warning is returned here.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-imperfect-proof-testing', 1, 'A proof test reveals only a fraction of the dangerous undetected failures. Where do the rest of them wait, on the engine''s treatment?', '["They are revealed at the next proof test like every other dangerous undetected failure, so coverage works out as a small correction to the interval and nothing more.", "They stay until the item is restored as new at the lifetime T2, so the uncovered share is averaged over the lifetime.", "They are charged against the MTTR, since a failure the test missed is found by the diagnostics soon after.", "They are dropped, because a failure no test reveals cannot be counted in an average."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s declared choice is that uncovered failures stay until the item is restored as new, which is why a coverage below one has to arrive with a lifetime. Treating them as revealed at the next test would make coverage a small correction and would remove the floor entirely. They are undetected failures, so the MTTR does not apply, and dropping them would understate the PFDavg.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-imperfect-proof-testing', 2, 'What is the FLOOR of a partially tested subsystem, in the sense the engine uses when it reports one?', '["The PFDavg the subsystem approaches as the proof test interval goes toward zero, with every other input held, so it is what no interval can reach below.", "The lowest PFDavg reached anywhere in the sensitivity sweep the analyst ran.", "The PFDavg at the lifetime, which is the longest interval worth asking about.", "The lowest PFDavg any coverage could produce on that subsystem."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The floor is the PFDavg as T1 goes to zero: the uncovered failures stay down for half the lifetime on average whatever the test interval. A sweep only samples intervals, the lifetime is the other end of the range entirely, and the floor is quoted at one stated coverage and moves when the coverage moves.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-imperfect-proof-testing', 3, 'The OBAGI shutdown valve is read at five coverages against a target of 0.02. What floor does the engine report at a coverage of 0.7?', '["0.007905600000, which is the floor the engine reports at a coverage of 0.8", "0.003963600000, which is the floor the engine reports at a coverage of 0.9", "0.001992600000, which is the floor the engine reports at a coverage of 0.95", "0.011847600000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The printed floor at a coverage of 0.7 is 0.011847600000. 0.007905600000, 0.003963600000 and 0.001992600000 are the floors at coverages of 0.8, 0.9 and 0.95, each one row off. The floor rises as the coverage falls, because more of the failures are waiting for the overhaul.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-imperfect-proof-testing', 4, 'At a perfect proof test the same valve still has a floor, of 0.000021600000. What is left in the answer once the interval has gone to zero?', '["The detected failures sitting down for their restoration time, which no proof test interval ever touches on any channel at all.", "The uncovered share of the undetected failures, which is small at a perfect test and never quite reaches zero on real equipment.", "The restoration time after each test, which is charged on every undetected failure however often the test runs.", "A numerical residue of the bisection, which converges to one part in a trillion and leaves a small remainder behind it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'At a coverage of one there is no uncovered share at all, so the floor of 0.000021600000 is only the MRT after each test. This channel has no detected failures, so no MTTR term is in the answer, and the floor is an exact limit of the form, which the search plays no part in.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-imperfect-proof-testing', 5, 'Take the floor from a perfect test to a coverage of 0.95 on that valve. What happens, and what does the step teach?', '["It goes from 0.000021600000 to 0.003963600000, which is in fact the floor the engine prints one row further down the table, at a coverage of 0.9 on the same valve.", "It goes from 0.000021600000 to 0.001992600000, so a small hole in the procedure buys a large floor because the failures behind it wait half a lifetime.", "It does not move, because the floor is set by the restoration time and the coverage only moves the answer at long intervals.", "It falls, because a coverage below one lets more of the failures be found at the overhaul."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The printed floors are 0.000021600000 at a perfect test and 0.001992600000 at a coverage of 0.95; 0.003963600000 belongs to a coverage of 0.9, one row further down. The uncovered share is multiplied by half the lifetime, which is far longer than the restoration time a perfect test leaves behind, so five percent of the failures moving into that share dominates the answer. The floor rises as the coverage falls and is not fixed by the restoration time alone.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-imperfect-proof-testing', 6, 'The floor of that valve at a coverage of 0.7 can be built from its stated inputs. Which expression gives it?', '["lambdaDU (1 - PTC)(T1/2 + MRT) + lambdaDU PTC MRT, which is the same split with the interval in place of the lifetime in the uncovered term.", "lambdaDU (T2/2 + MRT), which charges every undetected failure against the lifetime whatever the coverage happens to be.", "lambdaDU (1 - PTC)(T2/2 + MRT) + lambdaDU PTC MRT, which comes to 0.011847600000 and carries no T1 at all.", "lambdaDU PTC (T2/2 + MRT), which charges the covered share of the failures against the lifetime."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The digest derives the floor as lambdaDU (1 - PTC)(T2/2 + MRT) + lambdaDU PTC MRT and states that it is the engine''s floor to the digit. Nothing about T1 appears in it, which is exactly why no interval reaches below it. Putting T1 into the uncovered term, or charging every failure or the covered share against the lifetime, all misplace the split.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-imperfect-proof-testing', 7, 'The same valve is searched against a target of 0.02 at a coverage of 0.9. What longest interval does the engine return?', '["42122.573099 hours, or 4.808513 years, which is what a coverage of 0.95 supports against the same target", "39596.049383 hours, which is 4.520097 years", "33595.555556 hours, or 3.835109 years", "25880.634921 hours, or 2.954410 years"]'::jsonb, 1, 'At a coverage of 0.9 the engine returns 39596.049383 hours, 4.520097 years. 42122.573099 hours belongs to a coverage of 0.95, 33595.555556 hours to 0.8 and 25880.634921 hours to 0.7, so each wrong figure is the right search at the wrong coverage.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-imperfect-proof-testing', 8, 'With a perfect proof test, how many years may that valve run between tests before it passes the target of 0.02?', '["5.305186 years, which the engine returns for the IDU valves against a target of 0.01", "4.808513 years, which is the answer at a coverage of 0.95", "The whole ten year lifetime, because a perfect test has no uncovered share to cap it", "5.068087 years"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A perfect test gives 44396.444444 hours, 5.068087 years. 5.305186 years is the IDU valve answer against a target of 0.01 and belongs to another subsystem. 4.808513 years is the coverage 0.95 row. The lifetime would come back only with the state CAPPED_AT_LIFETIME, and this search found a crossing.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-imperfect-proof-testing', 9, 'A target is set below a partially tested subsystem''s floor. What does the longest interval search return, and what moves the floor?', '["No interval and the state UNACHIEVABLE, and only a better test or a shorter lifetime moves the floor, since more frequent testing of the same procedure moves nothing.", "A very short interval with the state FOUND, since shortening the test is always the lever that reaches a tighter target.", "The lifetime with the state CAPPED_AT_LIFETIME, since the floor and the lifetime are the same bound under two names.", "A refusal naming the target field, since a target below the floor cannot be met."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A target below the floor is UNACHIEVABLE whatever the interval, and the digest names the two things that move a floor: a better test or a shorter lifetime. No finite interval exists, so FOUND is impossible, and the lifetime cap is a different case where the target IS met. A refusal is a finding about the call, and this call is well formed.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-imperfect-proof-testing', 10, 'A 1oo1 at a coverage of 0.9 with a lifetime of 87600 hours meets a target of 0.01 even at the lifetime. What does the engine return, and why that number?', '["A very long interval with the state FOUND, because the bisection keeps going until the PFDavg rises to meet the target somewhere above the lifetime.", "No interval and the state UNACHIEVABLE, because a coverage of 0.9 leaves an uncovered share and an uncovered share always defeats a target.", "The lifetime itself with the state CAPPED_AT_LIFETIME, because no interval longer than the lifetime is meaningful.", "A refusal, because a search whose target is met everywhere has no crossing for the engine to report."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The target is met at the lifetime, so the engine reports the lifetime and the state CAPPED_AT_LIFETIME. Searching past the lifetime would return a combination the same engine declines, since it refuses a lifetime shorter than the interval. The target is met, so UNACHIEVABLE is wrong, and the call itself is well formed so nothing is refused.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-imperfect-proof-testing', 11, 'A site runs a partial stroke test at a short interval and a full test at the overhaul. How does the engine model that scheme?', '["As two separate test tasks at two separate intervals, each one carrying its own coverage, summed together into a single equivalent down time for the channel.", "As a coverage that improves with each test, so that the uncovered share shrinks over the life of the equipment and the floor falls with it.", "As a shorter effective lifetime, since a partial test at a short interval restores part of the item as new at that interval.", "As one coverage split: the covered part sees the short interval and the uncovered part sees the overhaul, and it models nothing more elaborate."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine models exactly one split, the covered part against the interval and the uncovered part against the lifetime, and nothing more elaborate. Two tasks at two intervals, an improving coverage and a shortened lifetime are all schemes an analyst must reduce to one coverage, one interval and one lifetime before the engine sees them, and the reduction is the analyst''s argument to record.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-imperfect-proof-testing', 12, 'A subsystem is typed with a proof test coverage below one and no lifetime. What does the engine say?', '["It declines and names the field, in its own words: lifetimeHours: is required when proofTestCoverage is below 1: the uncovered failures stay until the item is restored as new", "It assumes a ten year lifetime, which is the ordinary assumed life of process instrumentation.", "It treats the uncovered failures as revealed at the next proof test and returns a result.", "It returns a result with a warning that the uncovered share could not be averaged."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The message above is the engine''s own, and it names the field `lifetimeHours`. A refusal replaces the result entirely, so no assumed lifetime is supplied and no warned result comes back. The engine invents no number, which is why the lifetime has to be typed whenever coverage is below one.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-imperfect-proof-testing', 13, 'A lifetime shorter than the proof test interval is typed. Which reply does the engine give, word for word?', '["lifetimeHours: is required when proofTestCoverage is below 1: the uncovered failures stay until the item is restored as new", "lifetimeHours: must be at least the proof test interval", "proofTestIntervalHours: must be no longer than the lifetime, so the two inputs were swapped before the PFDavg was computed", "lifetimeHours: must be a time above 0 hours"]'::jsonb, 1, 'That is the engine''s own refusal, and it names `lifetimeHours`. The message saying a lifetime is required answers a coverage below one with no lifetime at all. The engine swaps nothing and computes nothing, because a refusal replaces the result and carries no number of its own.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-imperfect-proof-testing', 14, 'A proof test coverage of zero arrives. Which field does the engine name, and what range does it give?', '["proofTestCoverage, which must lie in [0, 1], so a coverage of zero is accepted and every undetected failure waits for the lifetime", "lifetimeHours, because a coverage of zero sends every undetected failure to the lifetime and the lifetime must then be checked first", "proofTestCoverage, which must lie in (0, 1]", "proofTestIntervalHours, which must be above 0"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine refuses in its own words, proofTestCoverage: must lie in (0, 1], so a coverage has to lie above zero and no higher than one. Nothing is accepted and no other field is named, because the coverage is checked on its own before the lifetime and a refusal replaces the result.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-imperfect-proof-testing', 15, 'Shortening the proof test interval is the cheapest lever an operating site has. Why does imperfect coverage make that lever run out?', '["The engine caps the shortest interval it will search at a fraction of the lifetime, so a site cannot drive the interval as far down as it would like to.", "The covered share does improve as the interval shortens, while the uncovered share does not move at all, so the PFDavg approaches a floor and never approaches zero.", "Each test carries its own restoration time, so testing more often raises the MTTR term faster than it lowers the undetected term.", "The beta factor rises as the interval falls, so a shorter interval buys less common cause reduction than it appears to buy."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The uncovered failures are waiting for the overhaul and the proof test never touches them, so driving the interval toward zero leaves the floor. The engine puts no lower cap on the interval it will search, the restoration time after a test is part of the floor and does not grow with testing, and the beta factor is a typed input that the interval does not move.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-how-conservative-annex-b-is', 1, 'The oracle behind this engine''s golden computes every case a second way. What is that second route?', '["The time dependent unavailability of the subsystem, averaged over the interval by numerical quadrature, with nothing linearised and no equivalent down time in it.", "The same Annex B equations evaluated a second time in exact rational arithmetic.", "The simplified forms evaluated with the detected rate and the MRT both set to zero.", "A Markov model of the subsystem solved for its steady state availability."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The golden records a time dependent route: the unavailability as a function of time, averaged by quadrature, with nothing linearised. Recomputing the same equations would check the typing and nothing else. Zeroing the detected rate and the MRT gives the simplified forms, which are a special case of the same algebra, and no Markov solution is carried in the golden.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-how-conservative-annex-b-is', 2, 'Across the golden cases read both ways, how does the engine sit against the time dependent route, and why?', '["Above it on the redundant architectures and below it on the single channels, because the equivalent down time convention cuts both ways depending on how many channels share a failure.", "At or above it in all 28 cases, because the forms are first order in the failure rate times the interval and use equivalent down times, and both choices err high.", "Below it in all 28 cases, because the linearisation drops a positive term of second order.", "Above it in most cases and below it in a few, with no stated direction to the error."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The digest states that across 28 golden cases the engine sits at or above route B every time, and names the two reasons. There is no case in the set where the engine falls below, so a mixed direction or a direction that depends on the architecture is wrong. Being below everywhere would be the unconservative direction and is the opposite of what is measured.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-how-conservative-annex-b-is', 3, 'Which golden case departs furthest from the time dependent route, and by how much?', '["dolan-valve-1oo2-ptc85, at 2.862e-2, which is the largest departure among the cases carrying imperfect coverage.", "tr84-2oo2, at 1.171e-2, which is the case whose published form carries no common cause term at all.", "1oo1-long-interval, at 15.12 percent, on a case whose failure rate times interval is 0.438000.", "2oo3-beta0, at 2.209e-2, the largest of the cases with no common cause."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The largest departure printed is 1oo1-long-interval at 15.12 percent, and its failure rate times interval is 0.438000, well past the 0.1 at which the engine warns. The coverage valve at 2.862e-2, the 2oo2 at 1.171e-2 and the 2oo3 with no common cause at 2.209e-2 are all real departures from the same table and all smaller.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-how-conservative-annex-b-is', 4, 'The published valve subsystem returns 0.001048767640 from the engine and 0.001043789195 on the time dependent route. What departure is printed against it?', '["8.000e-4, which is the departure printed against the pressure transmitter 2oo3", "2.862e-2, which is the departure printed against the same valve at reduced coverage", "1.171e-2, which is the departure printed against the 2oo2 case of the golden", "4.770e-3"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The printed departure for that pair is 4.770e-3. 8.000e-4 belongs to the pressure transmitter 2oo3 with a perfect test, 2.862e-2 to the same valve at reduced coverage and 1.171e-2 to the 2oo2 case, so each wrong figure is a real row read against the wrong case.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-how-conservative-annex-b-is', 5, 'The pressure transmitter 2oo3 departs by 8.000e-4 with a perfect test and by 9.482e-3 once its coverage falls. Which departure explains the growth?', '["The linearisation of the single channel form, which drops a term of second order in the failure rate times the interval and therefore leaves the answer high.", "The coverage cross term, which Annex B charges more generously than the exact average does whenever the lifetime is at least the interval.", "The missing common cause term for a 2oo2, which counts a failure of both channels twice.", "The bisection tolerance, which is one part in a trillion and grows with the lifetime."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The coverage cross term is charged at two thirds of the interval times the lifetime where the exact average charges half the interval times the lifetime plus a sixth of the interval squared, so the coverage cases are conservative by construction. The linearised single channel term and the 2oo2 omission are the other two departures and neither is at work in a 2oo3 with coverage. Bisection is not involved in a PFDavg at all.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-how-conservative-annex-b-is', 6, 'How does Annex B charge the coverage cross term, and what does the exact average charge?', '["Annex B charges two thirds of the interval times the lifetime, where the exact average charges half the interval times the lifetime plus a sixth of the interval squared.", "Annex B charges half the lifetime and the exact average charges half the interval.", "Annex B charges the whole lifetime and the exact average charges two thirds of it.", "Both charge the same term, and the coverage cases depart for a different reason."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The digest derives the departure exactly this way, and notes that the exact charge is lower whenever the lifetime is at least the interval, which the engine requires it to be. Halving or taking the whole lifetime misstates both sides, and the coverage cases do depart, by 2.862e-2 on the valve at reduced coverage against 4.770e-3 with a perfect test.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-how-conservative-annex-b-is', 7, 'Which of these is one of the four places the published forms depart from the exact average?', '["The band table rounds an exact decade upward, which pushes a subsystem sitting on a decade into the higher band and raises the reported figure.", "The series sum ignores the overlap between subsystems, which makes the summed function lower than the exact one and hides part of the conservatism.", "The equivalent down time convention charges detected failures and the MRT more than the exact average does.", "The bisection converges to one part in a trillion, which leaves a residue in every interval the engine returns."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The four departures are the linearised single channel term, the equivalent down time convention, the coverage cross term and the missing 2oo2 common cause term. The decade rule is a band convention and moves no PFDavg. The sum does ignore a small overlap, which is second order at these values and is not one of the four. Bisection belongs to the interval search.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-how-conservative-annex-b-is', 8, 'Why does Annex B overstate a 2oo2, on the counting the digest gives?', '["A 2oo2 needs both channels to fail before it fails, so every common cause failure is counted only once where two independent failures would have been counted twice over.", "A 2oo2 fails on the first dangerous failure of either channel, so a failure that takes out both at once is counted twice where it should be counted once.", "A 2oo2 carries a beta factor term that is applied to both channels, so the common cause share of the answer enters the sum a second time.", "A 2oo2 uses the group equivalent down time, which is longer than the channel equivalent down time."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The 2oo2 trips only when both channels work, so it fails on the first dangerous failure of either and its PFDavg is twice one channel''s. A common cause failure of both is no worse than one channel failing, so counting every failure as independent charges it twice, which errs high. Annex B carries no beta factor term for a 2oo2 at all, and a 2oo2 uses the channel equivalent down time.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-how-conservative-annex-b-is', 9, 'At a beta factor of 0.1 the golden''s time dependent route gives an exact 2oo2 average of 0.016460844922 against the Annex B figure. What is that Annex B figure, and how far above the exact average does it sit?', '["0.010576000000, which is 6.43 percent above it", "0.017317146501, which is 1.171e-2 above it", "0.017520000000, which is 15.12 percent above it", "0.017520000000, which is 6.43 percent above it"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The printed pair is 0.017520000000 against 0.016460844922, derived 6.43 percent above it. 0.010576000000 is the full channel 2oo2 PFDavg from a different set of inputs. 0.017317146501 is the time dependent value of a different 2oo2 case in the departure table. 15.12 percent is the largest departure in the whole set and belongs to the long interval single channel.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-how-conservative-annex-b-is', 10, 'What may a learner claim about the SIZE of the 2oo2 overstatement?', '["Only the measured 6.43 percent, because the counting fixes the direction of the error while no closed form for its size is taught or graded in this course at all.", "The beta factor times the Annex B figure, which is the share counted twice.", "Half the common cause term, since each of the two channels carries half of it.", "The difference between the group and the channel equivalent down times."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The digest prints the counting mechanism and the measured 6.43 percent, and that is all. A closed form would have to be derived rather than read, and none is published here, so none of the three expressions above has a printed line behind it. The measured figure also carries the linearisation, so it is the total conservatism of that case.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-how-conservative-annex-b-is', 11, 'On a subsystem with a perfect proof test, where does the engine''s rare event warning fire?', '["When the computed PFDavg goes above 0.1, which is the top of the SIL 1 band and the point at which no band can be claimed for the subsystem any more.", "When the interval goes above the lifetime, because the uncovered failures then have no end date to be averaged against at all.", "When the undetected failure rate times the interval goes above 0.1, where the overstatement becomes large enough to mention.", "When the computed PFDavg reaches one, at which point the answer has stopped being a probability."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The warning threshold is the undetected failure rate times the interval above 0.1, and with a coverage below one the engine forms that product with the lifetime in place of the interval. A PFDavg of one or more meets the REFUSAL boundary, which sits beyond the warning, and an interval above the lifetime is refused on its own field. The band ceiling is a separate matter and does not raise a warning.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-how-conservative-annex-b-is', 12, 'Past the warning is the refusal. Where does the engine stop answering, and what does it say?', '["At a computed PFDavg of one or more, with its own words: proofTestIntervalHours: the simplified equations give 4.38 here, which is not a probability: lambda x T is far outside the rare-event range they assume; use an exact (Markov) model", "At a computed PFDavg above 0.1, where the warning already said the answer was generous.", "At the interval where the departure from the time dependent route reaches 15.12 percent.", "At any interval longer than the lifetime that was typed for the subsystem."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The refusal boundary is a computed PFDavg of one or more, and the message quoted above is the engine''s own. Between the warning and the refusal the engine keeps answering and keeps saying the answer overstates. The departure from the time dependent route is provenance and steers no refusal, and a lifetime shorter than the interval is refused on a different field.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-how-conservative-annex-b-is', 13, 'How does a warning differ from a refusal in the verification half?', '["A warning is returned with a result and no field name, while a refusal is returned with a result and a field name, so both carry a figure a note can quote.", "A warning is returned WITH a result and says the answer overstates, while a refusal replaces the result entirely and carries no number.", "A warning names a field and a refusal names a function, and both of them come back alongside a computed PFDavg for the subsystem.", "A warning is raised by the oracle and a refusal by the engine."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A warning arrives with a result, and a refusal replaces it with an object carrying an error and the field that was wrong. A refusal carries no number, so it cannot be quoted as a figure. The oracle raises nothing at run time: it wrote the golden.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-how-conservative-annex-b-is', 14, 'Four golden cases and their printed departures: 1oo2-beta0 at 1.321e-2, 1oo2-ccf-dominated at 2.184e-4, 1oo3-beta0 at 2.121e-2 and 2oo3-beta0. Which of the four departs furthest from the time dependent route?', '["1oo2-beta0, at 1.321e-2, the largest of the four on the printed table", "1oo3-beta0, at 2.121e-2, the largest of the four on the printed table", "1oo2-ccf-dominated, at 2.184e-4, the largest of the four on the printed table", "2oo3-beta0, at 2.209e-2"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The printed departures are 1.321e-2, 2.184e-4, 2.121e-2 and 2.209e-2, so the 2oo3 with no common cause departs furthest of the four. The common cause dominated 1oo2 departs least of them by a wide margin, because its answer is carried by a single channel term that the linearisation barely touches.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-how-conservative-annex-b-is', 15, 'Why is a second ROUTE worth building, and what may its values be used for?', '["It repeats the published equations in higher precision, so a disagreement shows where the engine lost digits, and its values may be quoted as the achieved figure of a subsystem in a note.", "It replaces the published forms wherever the two disagree, so a site is assessed against whichever of the two routes gives the lower figure for the function.", "It starts from a different description of the same situation, so a disagreement means something; its values are provenance and are never graded.", "It proves the published forms correct wherever the two agree, so any agreeing row may be graded on either route."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A second route arrives at the same quantity by different arithmetic, so it can disagree in a way that means something, and here it disagrees by a small consistent amount with an explanation. The time dependent values are recorded beside the golden as evidence about the published forms and no capstone grades one. A site is assessed against the published forms, so a note that reports a time dependent value has left the standard behind.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-the-published-example-and-its-inferences', 1, 'How many inputs had to be inferred before the published table reproduced, and which were they?', '["Three: the restoration time after a proof test taken equal to the detected restoration time, a lifetime of ten years in the coverage table, and a beta factor of 15 percent where 10 percent is printed.", "Two: the lifetime in the coverage table and the beta factor of the transmitters.", "One: the lifetime, which the paper''s coverage table does not print anywhere.", "None: every input the reproduction used is printed in the source table."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The digest states that three inputs had to be INFERRED. Stopping at the lifetime and the beta factor leaves out the restoration time after a proof test, which the valve row needs. Stopping at the lifetime alone leaves out two. The reproduction is the reason the inferences were needed at all, so none is not an option.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-the-published-example-and-its-inferences', 2, 'The published valve subsystem is computed with the restoration time after a proof test set equal to its detected restoration time of 120 hours. Does the row reproduce, and what does that settle?', '["It misses the printed 1.05E-03 at the third figure, and only the row with that time set to zero reproduces, so the table used an MRT of zero throughout.", "It reproduces the printed 1.05E-03 to three figures, and setting that time to zero does not, so the table used MRT equal to the MTTR.", "It reproduces, and so does the row with that time set to zero, so the restoration time after a test is too short to matter on this subsystem.", "It misses until the beta factor is raised to 0.15."]'::jsonb, 1, 'With the two restoration times equal the engine returns 0.001048767640, which rounds to the printed 1.05E-03. With the restoration time after a test set to zero it returns 0.001017167254, which rounds to 1.02E-03 and misses at the third figure. That is the first of the three inferences: the published table reproduces only with MRT equal to the MTTR. The beta factor slip belongs to the transmitter coverage row and does not touch the valve.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-the-published-example-and-its-inferences', 3, 'Suppose the published valve row meant no restoration time after a test at all. What does the engine return for it then?', '["0.001048767640, which is what the same row gives with that time at 120 hours", "0.000676060358, which is what the transmitter coverage row gives at a beta factor of 0.15", "0.000344085754, which is what the transmitter row gives at a beta factor of 0.15", "0.001017167254"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The row at zero returns 0.001017167254, which is 1.02E-03 against a printed 1.05E-03. 0.001048767640 is the same row at 120 hours, and the other two figures belong to the pressure transmitters, one of them in the coverage table and one of them without coverage.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-the-published-example-and-its-inferences', 4, 'Where are the three inferences recorded, and what does that do to the count?', '["The golden''s source lines record all three of them, so the count is three and the engine''s validation record adds nothing at all to what the golden already carries in its own lines.", "The golden records two of them and the engine''s validation record holds a third item which is no inference at all, so the count of genuinely inferred inputs comes to two.", "The golden''s source lines record two of them and the engine''s validation record holds the restoration time inference, and the count of inferred inputs is still three.", "The engine''s source comments record all three, which is why the digest re-measures them."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The digest says the golden''s source lines record the lifetime and the beta factor slip, while the restoration time inference sits in the engine''s validation record and is shown by running the row both ways. Where a record lives does not change what was inferred, so the count is three. The engine''s own comments are provenance and record none of this.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-the-published-example-and-its-inferences', 5, 'Why does the restoration time inference show on the valve subsystem in particular?', '["Its architecture is a 1oo2, and a redundant subsystem charges the restoration time after a proof test twice over, once in each of the two channels of the pair.", "Its restoration time is long, at 120 hours, so setting the time after a proof test to zero moves the row far enough to change the printed third figure.", "It is the only subsystem in the published table that carries any detected failures at all, so it is the only row where a restoration time enters the arithmetic.", "It is the only subsystem whose proof test interval differs from the one year used elsewhere."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The valve row carries an MTTR and an MRT of 120 hours, which is long, and the digest computes the row both ways to show the effect. The published table uses a proof test interval of one year throughout, and several of its subsystems carry detected failures, so neither of those makes the valve special. A redundant architecture does not double charge a restoration time.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-the-published-example-and-its-inferences', 6, 'The coverage table''s lifetime is not printed. What did the sweep of lifetimes show, and what is the honest label for the result?', '["Only a lifetime of ten years reproduces all four printed coverage rows in the sweep, which is strong evidence short of proof, so the golden records the value as INFERRED.", "Every lifetime in the sweep reproduces at least one row, so no lifetime can be preferred.", "A lifetime of eight years reproduces all four rows and is recorded as a measured input.", "The sweep was inconclusive, so the coverage rows were abandoned as unreproducible."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Four rows match at ten years and at least one row misses at every other lifetime in the sweep, so the inference is strong. It is not proof, because some other combination could in principle land on the same four figures and only five lifetimes were tried, and the golden carries the word INFERRED with the value. Eight years misses on the valve and on the transmitters.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-the-published-example-and-its-inferences', 7, 'The pressure transmitter coverage row prints 6.76E-04. What does the engine give for it at a lifetime of eight years?', '["4.89E-04, which is what the same row gives at a lifetime of five years", "7.53E-04, which is what the same row gives at a lifetime of twelve years", "8.70E-04, which is what the same row gives at a lifetime of fifteen years", "6.00E-04"]'::jsonb, 3, 'At eight years the transmitter coverage row gives 6.00E-04 against a printed 6.76E-04, so it misses at the second figure. 4.89E-04, 7.53E-04 and 8.70E-04 are the five, twelve and fifteen year columns of the same row, each one of the lifetimes the sweep tried and none of them reproducing.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-the-published-example-and-its-inferences', 8, 'Why is agreement on four rows a stronger argument than agreement on one?', '["The four rows are recomputed from one another, so agreement in one of them propagates through the remaining three and multiplies the strength of the evidence.", "A single figure can be hit by accident, and four figures give four chances to hit one by accident, which is what makes the argument strong.", "The four are different subsystems with different coverages and rates, and at least one of them misses the printed value in every other lifetime column.", "Four rows exhaust the published table, so nothing is left that could disagree."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A single figure can be hit by accident, which is why one row would prove little; four independent subsystems agreeing at one lifetime and failing elsewhere is what carries the argument. Each row is computed from its own inputs, more chances to hit by accident would weaken the case, and the published table carries five subsystems in its main results.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-the-published-example-and-its-inferences', 9, 'Reading the valve row of the coverage sweep across its lifetime columns, at which lifetime does it read 3.78E-03?', '["Five years, the column in which that valve row reads 1.75E-03", "Eight years, the column in which that valve row reads 2.31E-03", "Twelve years, the column in which that valve row reads 3.13E-03", "Fifteen years"]'::jsonb, 3, '3.78E-03 stands in the fifteen year column. The five, eight and twelve year columns of that row read 1.75E-03, 2.31E-03 and 3.13E-03, and the printed 2.71E-03 is reproduced only in the ten year column.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-the-published-example-and-its-inferences', 10, 'The transmitter coverage row prints a beta factor of 10 percent. Computed with the beta factor and betaD at that printed value, what does the engine return?', '["0.000676060358, which is 6.76E-04 at three significant figures and reproduces the printed value exactly.", "0.000473588152, which is 4.74E-04 at three significant figures against a printed 6.76E-04.", "0.000344085754, which is 3.44E-04 and reproduces a different printed row of the same paper.", "0.001017167254, which is 1.02E-03 and misses its printed value at the third figure."]'::jsonb, 1, 'At the printed beta factor the engine returns 0.000473588152, which rounds to 4.74E-04 and misses the printed 6.76E-04 by far more than rounding can explain. 0.000676060358 is the same row at a beta factor of 0.15. 0.000344085754 in the raised beta factor table belongs to the transmitters without coverage, and 0.001017167254 to the valve with its restoration time after a test set to zero.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-the-published-example-and-its-inferences', 11, 'At what beta factor does that transmitter coverage row reproduce, and with what figure?', '["At 0.1, the printed value, where the engine returns 0.000473588152 and the printed figure follows once the third figure is rounded the other way.", "At 0.02, the beta factor of the other two coverage rows, where the row returns its printed value without any adjustment at all.", "At a beta factor and betaD of 0.15, where the engine returns 0.000676060358, which is 6.76E-04 at three significant figures.", "At 0.1, with the lifetime lengthened to fifteen years to make up the difference."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The row reproduces at a beta factor of 0.15, returning 0.000676060358 and so 6.76E-04. At the printed 0.1 the engine gives 0.000473588152, which is too far off for rounding. The other coverage rows carry their own beta factors and do not fix this one, and a longer lifetime is a separate inference that the four row sweep already settled at ten years.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-the-published-example-and-its-inferences', 12, 'What is the evidence that 15 percent is the value the published coverage table actually used?', '["The table is headed as one that applies the MooN multiplication to the beta factor, which raises 10 percent by half to 15 percent, so the heading names the operation that turns the printed figure into the reproducing one.", "The paper prints 15 percent in a second table, so the coverage table was read across.", "A beta factor of 0.15 is the usual value for voted transmitters in the process sector.", "The oracle''s time dependent route reproduces the row only at a beta factor of 0.15."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The heading of the table describes the MooN multiplication, which takes 10 percent to 15 percent, and that is the independent explanation from the document itself. The paper prints 10 percent on the row in question, the course recommends no beta factor as usual practice, and the time dependent route is provenance about the forms and settles nothing about an input.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-the-published-example-and-its-inferences', 13, 'A second published row confirms the beta factor reading. Which row, and what does it return?', '["The valve subsystem at a beta factor of 0.15, which returns 0.001048767640 and so reproduces the printed 1.05E-03 that the restoration time inference had already accounted for.", "The pressure transmitter row of the raised beta factor table, which reproduces its printed 3.44E-04 with the beta factor and betaD both at 0.15.", "The analogue input card in the raised table, which reproduces at 0.15 and is recorded in the golden.", "The summed function, whose total moves to the printed value once the beta factor is raised."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The transmitter row of the raised beta factor table returns 0.000344085754 at 0.15 and reproduces its printed 3.44E-04, so two independent rows reproduce at 15 percent, and neither printed figure reproduces at 10 percent. The valve carries its own beta factor and its own inference. The analogue input card is the row the golden leaves out, and no total is offered as evidence here.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-the-published-example-and-its-inferences', 14, 'One further row of the raised beta factor table was left out of the golden. Which, and why?', '["The valve row, whose restoration time after a proof test is inferred rather than printed, so it could not serve as evidence about a beta factor at the same time.", "The logic solver row, which carries no common cause term at all and therefore cannot test a beta factor reading of any kind.", "The analogue input card, which reproduces only with a betaD that looks like a rounded print, so it is too ambiguous to stand as evidence.", "The summed total, which rounds to its printed value at either beta factor."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The golden leaves out the analogue input card in the raised table, because it reproduces only with a detected common cause figure that looks like a rounded print. A reproduction argument is built from the rows that are clean, and the ambiguous row is named and set aside in the open. The other three are not what the digest records as left out.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-the-published-example-and-its-inferences', 15, 'What is a published table evidence for, once a reproduction has needed an inferred input?', '["It is evidence about the equations for the rows it reproduces, and the note has to say which input was inferred and what the alternative value produced.", "It is evidence about the failure rates it prints, which a reader may carry to another plant.", "It is evidence about nothing at all once any input has had to be inferred.", "It is evidence that the inferred input is the correct one for any similar subsystem."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The digest''s lesson for a verification note is that a reproduction which needs an inferred input says which input and why, and that a published table is evidence about the equations only for the rows it reproduces. The rates are the source''s own example values, the inference is about the source and travels no further, and an inference that is declared still supports the rows that reproduce.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-do', 1, 'Why does this engine carry no hardware fault tolerance check?', '["The requirement is a normative table of IEC 61511-1 and IEC 61508-2, and the engine does not restate a licensed table it could not check against a public source.", "The check is a special case of the PFDavg calculation and adds nothing.", "The check belongs to high demand functions and has no low demand form.", "The check was left out of the engine''s scope and is planned for a later release."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s header gives the two reasons together: the requirement lives in a normative table of a licensed standard and there was no public source to check a restatement against. It is a separate requirement and no part of the PFDavg arithmetic, it applies in low demand as well, and this engine has no repair history and nothing is pending on it.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-do', 2, 'What does the analyst do about the architectural constraint, and what does a passing PFDavg settle?', '["The analyst reads the constraint off the engine''s band, since a subsystem whose achieved figure sits inside the required band has already demonstrated the redundancy the constraint asks for on that band.", "The analyst checks the architecture against the standard separately, because a PFDavg that meets its target does not by itself satisfy the architectural constraint.", "The analyst takes the constraint as satisfied whenever the architecture is redundant.", "The analyst raises it only when the engine returns a warning about the architecture."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The digest states the action plainly: check the architecture against the standard separately, because a PFDavg that meets the target does not by itself satisfy the architectural constraint. The engine returns no band, no warning and no output of any kind about the constraint, so nothing in its answer can settle it, and redundancy alone is not the test the standard applies.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-do', 3, 'What does the engine do about high demand or continuous mode?', '["It computes a per hour figure alongside the PFDavg and reports whichever of the two the demand rate makes appropriate for the function in hand.", "It converts the PFDavg to a per hour figure by dividing by the proof test interval, which is the conversion the standard sets out for the purpose.", "Nothing at all: every band it reports is low demand, and it exports no per hour band and no function that would return one.", "It refuses any call whose demand rate is high, and names the demand rate field."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine''s header says every band here is low demand, and its exported names carry one band table and nothing else. There is no second table, no conversion and no demand rate input at all, so the engine cannot refuse on one either. That is what makes the wrong mode a quiet error rather than a loud one.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-do', 4, 'The demand rate on a function turns out to be high. What does the analyst do, on the engine''s own account of the limit?', '["Shortens the proof test interval until the low demand treatment applies again, which is what brings the demand rate back below the test rate on the function.", "Keeps the PFDavg and records the demand rate beside it as a caveat in the note.", "Raises the target PFDavg by the ratio of the demand rate to the test rate.", "Uses a PFH method, because a low demand PFDavg is the wrong quantity there."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The digest''s row says the analyst uses a PFH method when the demand rate is high, and that a low demand PFDavg is the wrong quantity there. A wrong quantity is not repaired by shortening an interval, by a caveat beside it or by scaling the target, because the average probability is no longer what characterises the function.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-do', 5, 'What failure rate data does the engine carry?', '["A small default library for valves, transmitters and logic solvers, used whenever the analyst leaves a rate blank on the subsystem.", "None: users supply every lambda, and the golden rates are labelled illustrative or cited to where they came from.", "The rates of the published worked example, which it reuses for any subsystem of the same architecture.", "The rates of IEC 61508 and the ISA technical reports, reproduced under citation."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine carries no failure rate data at all. Every lambda arrives as an input and the golden rates are labelled illustrative or cited. There is no default that fills a blank, the published rates belong to that source''s own example, and licensed tables are cited here and never reproduced.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-do', 6, 'The engine does not choose the TMEL, the initiating event frequency or an IPL PFD. What is the analyst''s duty on each?', '["Every one is an input, and the analyst may leave any of them blank, in which case the engine works from the remaining terms of the row and reports which ones it used.", "Every one is an input, and the engine checks each against the ranges the standard publishes before it uses the row.", "Every one is an input, and the analyst justifies each against the organisation''s criteria.", "Every one is derived by the engine from the scenario description supplied with the row."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The digest''s row reads that every one is an input and the analyst justifies each against the organisation''s criteria. The engine invents no number and derives none of these, it checks only the ranges its own refusals police, and a missing term is refused rather than worked around.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-do', 7, 'The engine forms no judgement about whether a layer is truly independent. What does it do, and what is left to the analyst?', '["It applies the credit flags exactly as they are typed, and the analyst audits each IPL against the initiating cause and against the other layers on the row.", "It compares the layers on the row and withholds credit from any that share a named device.", "It credits every layer and leaves the analyst to remove the ones that are not independent.", "It refuses a row whose layers have not been audited, and names the offending layer."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The credit rules are flags applied as given, and the analyst does the auditing against the initiating cause and the other layers. The engine cannot see that an alarm shares a transmitter with the initiating loop unless the analyst says so, so it neither compares devices nor refuses an unaudited row, and it withholds credit only where the flags say to.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-do', 8, 'Which sentence does the engine give, verbatim, as the rule for crediting an IPL?', '["An IPL is credited once, and only when flagged independent === true and also flagged auditable === true, so that a layer with no auditable flag at all goes uncredited.", "An IPL is credited once, and only when flagged independent === true and not flagged auditable === false.", "An IPL is credited once for each separate hazard scenario it protects against, provided that it is flagged independent on the row.", "An IPL is credited whenever its typed figure lies above zero and no higher than one, since a valid figure is what credit turns on."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The rule quoted above is the engine''s own. A missing auditable flag is not a false one, so a layer with no such flag can still be credited, which is what makes the rule exactly as narrow as it is. Credit is one per IPL whatever it protects, and a valid figure is a range check with no bearing on credit.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-do', 9, 'What evidence stands behind the IPL credit rules?', '["The independent oracle decides them in exact rational arithmetic, in the same way it decides every frequency product and every band boundary on a row.", "Two published sources state the same rules, which is the evidence behind the Annex B forms.", "No independent route validates them: they are the engine''s contract, pinned by behaviour tests, and the judgement stays with the analyst.", "The published worked example reproduces only when the rules are applied as typed."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine''s validation record says no independent route validates the credit rules. The oracle decides the LOPA arithmetic and the boundaries in exact rationals, the two published statements stand behind the Annex B equations, and the published reproduction is about the verification half, so none of the three reaches the credit rules.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-do', 10, 'What did the shared negative control on the credit rules show?', '["It removed the auditable exclusion from both the engine and the oracle at once and the whole suite stayed green, so the control cannot see that rule at all.", "It removed the auditable exclusion from the engine alone and the suite went red.", "It removed the auditable exclusion and the golden had to be rewritten to match.", "It changed the independence flag to a string and the engine refused the row."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The control removed the exclusion from both sides at once and the suite stayed green, because the oracle had no separate opinion to disagree with. A control that stays green when a rule is deleted is a control that cannot see the rule. Nothing went red, no golden was rewritten and the string flag is a separate behaviour the engine handles by withholding credit.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-do', 11, 'The engine has two halves. What are they, and what does each answer?', '["A determination half that chooses the tolerable frequency for the consequence, and a verification half that checks the chosen figure against the band table the standard publishes for low demand functions.", "A determination half that finds the risk reduction still missing against a tolerable frequency, and a verification half that computes the PFDavg a function achieves.", "A determination half that bands an achieved PFDavg, and a verification half that sums subsystems.", "A determination half for low demand functions and a verification half for high demand ones."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The determination half is a layer of protection analysis: a scenario frequency, the reduction still missing against a tolerable frequency and the band that reduction falls in. The verification half computes the PFDavg by the Annex B low demand equations. The engine chooses no tolerable frequency, banding and summing both live in the verification half, and there is no high demand half at all.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-do', 12, 'What do the engine''s functions return?', '["Either a result object carrying a basis block, or a null, with the offending input left for the caller to find by inspecting the arguments it passed.", "Always a result object, with any problem at all reported as a warning inside the basis block of the result it hands back.", "Either a result object or a thrown exception carrying both the message and the name of the input field that offended the engine.", "Either a result object carrying a basis block, or an object with an error and a field naming the offending input."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every function returns a result object with a basis block or an object with an error and a field, where the field names the offending input. A null would leave the caller to guess, a warning is returned only alongside a real result, and nothing is thrown, which is why a refusal can be quoted as a message and a field.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-do', 13, 'Which pair of exported constants does the engine carry?', '["DECADE_SNAP at 1e-9 and a default proof test interval of 8760 hours for a subsystem whose interval is left blank by the analyst who typed it.", "DECADE_SNAP at 1e-9 and a table of low demand failure rates for the five architectures.", "A default tolerable frequency and HOURS_PER_YEAR at 8760 hours in a year.", "DECADE_SNAP at 1e-9 and HOURS_PER_YEAR at 8760."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The exported constants include DECADE_SNAP at 1e-9 and HOURS_PER_YEAR at 8760, alongside the architecture list, the outcome states and the band table. There is no default interval, no rate table and no default tolerable frequency, because the engine invents no number.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-do', 14, 'What does `decadeOf` refuse?', '["A negative number and a string, which it refuses by naming the field, while anything off a decade comes back as the nearest decade within the snap.", "Anything that sits off a decade entirely, which it names as the offending input in the error object that it returns.", "Nothing at all: it returns null for zero, for a negative number, for a string and for anything off a decade.", "A value inside the snap of a decade, which it treats as ambiguous."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The digest says `decadeOf` refuses nothing and returns null in all four of those cases. A value inside the snap is exactly the case it DOES answer, by returning the power of ten. Nothing is named as an offending input, because there is no error object here at all.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-do', 15, 'The engine invents no number. Which inputs does that statement cover?', '["The initiating event frequency, every enabling condition and conditional modifier probability, every IPL PFD, every failure rate and the tolerable frequency, all of them typed.", "Every input except the tolerable frequency, which the engine derives from the band.", "Every input except the failure rates, which come from the vendored golden.", "Every input except the proof test interval, which defaults to one year."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s header lists them all as inputs: the initiating event frequency, every enabling condition and conditional modifier, every IPL PFD, every failure rate and the tolerable frequency. There is no exception in that list, so no band supplies a tolerance, the golden supplies no rate to a user''s call, and no interval defaults.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 1, 'A function must achieve 0.007407407407. Its transmitters take 0.000369505528 and its logic solver 0.000136440000, both on a one year test. What is left for the valves?', '["0.007407407407, because the requirement applies to each subsystem in its own right", "0.001792971954, which is what the whole function achieves on a one year test", "0.000369505528, which is only the transmitters'' share of the sum on a one year test", "0.006901461880"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A function is a series sum, so the requirement is a budget and the remainder after the two parts is 0.006901461880. The requirement applies to the sum and never to each part alone. 0.001792971954 is the achieved figure of the whole function on a one year test, and 0.000369505528 is the transmitters'' share alone, which is one of the two figures the remainder is found by taking away.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 2, 'That remainder of 0.006901461880 is handed to the longest interval search as the valve target. What comes back?', '["35393.040486 hours, which is 4.040301 years, with the state CAPPED_AT_LIFETIME, because the search has run out at the end of the equipment life and reports the cap.", "35393.040486 hours, which is 4.040301 years, with the state FOUND.", "46473.426980 hours, which is 5.305186 years, with the state FOUND.", "No interval and the state UNACHIEVABLE, because the remainder is smaller than the requirement."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine returns 35393.040486 hours, 4.040301 years, with the state FOUND. 46473.426980 hours belongs to the same valves against a target of 0.01, which is a looser figure than the remainder. A remainder smaller than the whole requirement is what a budget always produces and says nothing about achievability, and nothing here is capped.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 3, 'Why does the state FOUND matter as much as the interval itself when a budget is turned into a schedule?', '["It says a crossing exists, so the budget is reachable by scheduling alone and no new hardware is needed to meet the requirement on those valves.", "It says the search converged, which every successful search reports.", "It says the interval is inside the equipment lifetime and can be scheduled.", "It says the target was reached exactly, which the interval column also shows."]'::jsonb, 0, 'FOUND means the PFDavg rises to meet the target at a finite interval, so scheduling alone closes the budget. A state that only reported convergence would be worth nothing, since the engine returns a state on every call. Being inside the lifetime is what CAPPED_AT_LIFETIME reports on, and every FOUND row reaches its target exactly, which is a property of the search and gives no separate reason to record the state.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 4, 'The teaching function is re-verified with every proof test interval stretched to 3.5 years. What does it achieve, and what is the verdict?', '["0.006819960176, which is still SIL 2 and meets the requirement, since the band has not moved at all between the two stretches and the function is therefore unaffected.", "0.010066196546, which has dropped to SIL 1 and misses the requirement of 0.007407407407.", "0.008382016506, which is still SIL 2 and misses the requirement of 0.007407407407.", "0.008382016506, which has dropped to SIL 1 and misses the requirement."]'::jsonb, 2, 'At 3.5 years the function achieves 0.008382016506, which is above the required 0.007407407407, so it misses while it is still SIL 2. 0.006819960176 is the three year row and it meets. 0.010066196546 is the four year row. The drop to SIL 1 happens only at four years, which is the point of the row.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 5, 'The same function is stretched to four years. What is its achieved risk reduction factor and its band there?', '["119.303034 and SIL 2, which is what the function reports at a stretch of 3.5 years", "146.628422 and SIL 2, which is what the function reports at a stretch of three years", "246.170895 and SIL 2, which is what the function reports at a stretch of two years", "99.342388, in the SIL 1 band"]'::jsonb, 3, 'At four years the function achieves 0.010066196546, a risk reduction factor of 99.342388, and SIL 1. 119.303034, 146.628422 and 246.170895 belong to the 3.5 year, three year and two year rows, all of which are still SIL 2.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 6, 'At a stretch of three years, what does the teaching function report?', '["119.303034, and it no longer meets the requirement at that stretch", "99.342388, and it no longer meets the requirement at that stretch", "246.170895, and it still meets the requirement at that stretch", "146.628422, and it still meets the requirement"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Three years is the last stretch that still meets the tolerable frequency, with a PFDavg of 0.006819960176 and a risk reduction factor of 146.628422. The figures 119.303034 and 99.342388 go with the stretches of 3.5 and four years, where the function misses, and 246.170895 goes with two years.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 7, 'Read the band column of that stretch table alone. What would a reader miss?', '["That the function stopped meeting its requirement between 3.5 years and four years, which is where the band column changes and where the verdict column changes with it.", "That the function stopped meeting its requirement between three years and 3.5 years, while four successive rows all read SIL 2.", "Nothing, because the band and the verdict change on the same row of the table.", "That the achieved risk reduction factor falls faster than the interval grows."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The verdict turns false at 3.5 years while the band is still SIL 2, and the band falls to SIL 1 only at four years, so the two columns change on different rows. A SIL band is a decade wide and the required PFDavg is one number inside it, so a SIL that holds is no evidence that the requirement holds.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 8, 'Which three signals say that more of the same hardware will not close the gap?', '["A required risk reduction factor beyond SIL 3, a target below a coverage floor, and a common cause term that dominates every architecture on offer.", "A warning on the interval, a refusal on a field, and a band that has dropped.", "A long proof test interval, a high MTTR, and a low proof test coverage.", "A required PFDavg inside the band, an achieved figure above it, and a missed target."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Those three are the standing redesign signals, and the engine reports each as a state, a floor or a dominant term. A warning and a refusal are about a call and an input. A long interval, a high restoration time and a low coverage are inputs that can often be changed. A miss on its own is the ordinary case that scheduling or a better subsystem usually answers.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 9, 'On a redesign signal, what does the engine supply and what does it leave to the analyst?', '["It reports the state and also recommends the redesign, weighing the cost of new hardware against the cost of tightening the tolerable frequency on the row it was given.", "It reports nothing until the analyst asks for a redesign check, which is a separate call.", "It reports the state, the floor or the dominant term, and the decision to redesign is the analyst''s.", "It reports the state and refuses any further call on that subsystem until an input changes."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine reports each signal as a state, a floor or a dominant term, and the decision is the analyst''s. It does not weigh the cost of a redesign against a tighter tolerable frequency and has no opinion on whether a layer outside the instrumented system is available. There is no redesign call and no lock on further calls.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 10, 'Which set of items belongs in a verification note written to this course''s standard?', '["The scenario and its tolerable frequency, the required PFDavg as well as the band, every subsystem with its inputs and the source of each rate, the summed figure and its risk reduction factor, each interval with its state, any warning returned, and what the engine did not check.", "The achieved band, the verdict, and the date of the calculation.", "The achieved PFDavg and the required PFDavg, with nothing else needed.", "The inputs and the outputs of every engine call made, in the order they were made."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The digest lists the shape of the note item by item, and every one of them is there so a reader who disagrees can recompute the answer. A band and a verdict cannot be audited, a pair of figures leaves out the rates and the states, and a log of calls in order records the work without making the verification argument.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 11, 'Why does the note carry the required PFDavg as well as the band it falls in?', '["The band is used for the verdict and the required figure for the interval search, so the note needs one of them for each of the two decisions that have to be recorded in it.", "The band is a decade wide and the required figure is one number inside it, so writing only the bands would leave out the comparison that settles the verdict.", "The band is what the engine returns and the required figure is what the analyst assumes, so both have to be recorded to keep the two apart.", "The two are always the same band, so recording both is a check on the arithmetic."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A band covers a decade of PFDavg and the required figure is one number inside it, so a function whose band matches the required band can still miss. The verdict comes from comparing the two figures, the engine returns the required figure as well as the band, and the two bands matching is exactly the trap this tier exists to prevent.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 12, 'Why does a reported proof test interval carry its state?', '["The state records how long the bisection took to converge, which a reviewer needs in order to judge how much numerical confidence to place in the interval that was reported.", "The state records which architecture the subsystem used, which the interval alone does not reveal.", "FOUND, UNACHIEVABLE, INTERVAL_INDEPENDENT and CAPPED_AT_LIFETIME are very different findings arriving in the same field, and only the state tells them apart.", "The state records whether the interval was rounded to hours or to years."]'::jsonb, 2, 'An interval with no state beside it is ambiguous: a crossing, a target the design cannot reach, an answer the interval never entered, and a bound set by the lifetime all look alike as a number. The state carries none of the convergence history, the architecture or the rounding, all of which are recorded elsewhere in the note.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 13, 'The note has a heading for what the engine did not check. What belongs under it?', '["The subsystems that were not on the critical path of the function, and any interval that a planner has not yet agreed to with the maintenance team on the plant.", "The architectural constraint, the demand mode assumed low, and the fact that every failure rate is an input somebody has to justify.", "The golden cases that did not reproduce and the rows the oracle disagreed with.", "Any figure the engine returned with a warning attached to it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The limits the engine states are the architectural constraint, the high demand mode and the absence of failure rate data, together with the judgement of independence. A warning belongs under the caveats heading, since a warned figure IS a figure the engine checked. Every golden case reproduces, and the subsystems are all in the sum.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 14, 'Across the whole stretch from one year to four years, what happens to the achieved risk reduction factor of the teaching function?', '["It falls from 557.733208 to 119.303034, which is the lowest value the stretch table reaches before the function leaves the band it started in.", "It falls from 246.170895 to 99.342388, since the one year row is the reference and the two year row is where the stretch is first recorded.", "It falls from 557.733208 to 99.342388.", "It rises from 99.342388 to 557.733208."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The one year row reads 557.733208 and the four year row 99.342388. 119.303034 is the 3.5 year row and 246.170895 the two year row, so neither is an end of the stretch. The risk reduction factor is one over PFDavg, so it falls as the interval grows.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 15, 'What exactly was stretched in the table that carries those five rows?', '["Every proof test interval in the function at once, which is what a deferred turnaround does to a plant and is a harsher change than moving one subsystem alone.", "The valve interval alone, with the other two subsystems held at one year.", "The lifetime of every subsystem, with the proof test intervals held at one year.", "The interval of whichever subsystem carried the largest share of the sum."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The digest states that the function is re-verified with every proof test interval stretched together. Holding two subsystems still, moving a lifetime or moving only the largest share would each be a different and narrower question, with a different answer, which is why the note records which question was asked.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 1, 'The sensitivity sweep runs from a quarter of a year to eight years. What is its interval of 35040 hours in years?', '["4.000000 years, because the years figure is the hours over 8760 and HOURS_PER_YEAR is 8760.", "8.000000 years, because the sweep doubles at every step and 35040 hours is its last row.", "2.000000 years, which is the years figure printed against 17520 hours.", "Three years, which is one of the intervals the teaching function is re-verified at."]'::jsonb, 0, '35040 hours over 8760 is 4.000000 years. 8.000000 years belongs to 70080 hours, which is the last row of the sweep, and 2.000000 years to 17520 hours. Three years is a stretch of the teaching function and is not a row of this sweep at all.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 2, 'What does the EKULAMA full channel as a 1oo2 achieve at a proof test interval of 2190 hours?', '["0.000073541861, which is what the same channel achieves as a 2oo3 at that interval", "0.000141276391, which is what the same 1oo2 achieves at 4380 hours", "0.000068932620", "0.000300179812, which is what the IDU valves achieve at that interval"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The full 1oo2 reads 0.000068932620 at a quarter year interval. 0.000073541861 is the 2oo3 column on the same row, 0.000141276391 is the 1oo2 at twice the interval, and 0.000300179812 belongs to the valve column.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 3, 'The EKULAMA full 2oo3 has the fastest growing ratio column in the sweep. What does that column read at its last doubling?', '["2.689723, which is what the EKULAMA full 1oo2 reads at the same doubling", "3.222922", "2.657218, which is what the IDU valves read at the same doubling", "2.879660, which is what this same 2oo3 column reads one doubling earlier"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The 2oo3 ratio column ends at 3.222922. 2.689723 and 2.657218 are the 1oo2 and valve columns at the same row, and 2.879660 is the 2oo3 column at 35040 hours, one doubling earlier. The 2oo3 climbs fastest because the independent term, which grows with the square of T1, is the largest share of its PFDavg at one year: 0.000105777528 of 0.000369505528.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 4, 'Reading one row of the sweep across at 8760 hours, what do the EKULAMA full 1oo2 and full 2oo3 columns show?', '["0.000298987176 against 0.000369505528, so the 2oo3 buys PFDavg against the 1oo2 and is the better choice on the figures alone as the interval grows.", "0.000369505528 against 0.000298987176, so the 1oo2 costs PFDavg against the 2oo3.", "0.000068932620 against 0.000073541861, which is the same pair at a quarter of a year.", "0.000298987176 against 0.000369505528, so the 2oo3 costs PFDavg against the 1oo2 on this channel."]'::jsonb, 3, 'At 8760 hours the 1oo2 reads 0.000298987176 and the 2oo3 reads 0.000369505528, so the higher figure belongs to the 2oo3 and voting costs PFDavg here. The pair 0.000068932620 and 0.000073541861 is the quarter year row. What the voting buys is tolerance of a channel tripping spuriously, which this engine does not compute.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 5, 'Which interval does the longest interval search return for the IDU valves when their target is 0.01?', '["44396.444444 hours, or 5.068087 years, which is what a different subsystem supports against a looser target of its own", "46473.426980 hours, or 5.305186 years", "27640.443736 hours, or 3.155302 years, against a tighter target", "35393.040486 hours"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Against a target of 0.01 the valves support 46473.426980 hours, 5.305186 years. 44396.444444 hours is the OBAGI valve with a perfect test against a target of 0.02. 27640.443736 hours is the same IDU valves against 0.005, and 35393.040486 hours is the interval a budget remainder buys them.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 6, 'Which target PFDavg gives the IDU valves a longest interval of 27640.443736 hours?', '["0.002, which gives them 12957.474301 hours", "0.001, which gives them 6953.661742 hours", "0.01, which gives them 46473.426980 hours", "0.005"]'::jsonb, 3, '27640.443736 hours, 3.155302 years, is the answer against a target of 0.005. The targets 0.002, 0.001 and 0.01 give 12957.474301, 6953.661742 and 46473.426980 hours, so each wrong target has its own printed interval.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 7, 'Which two exported constants fix the units in which an interval is reported, and how?', '["HOURS_PER_YEAR at 8760 and DECADE_SNAP at 1e-9, which between them set the tolerance the bisection converges to when it reports an interval in hours.", "HOURS_PER_YEAR at 8760 alone, since the engine reports no band with an interval.", "HOURS_PER_YEAR at 8760, which turns the hours into the years column, with DECADE_SNAP at 1e-9 deciding only the banding.", "DECADE_SNAP at 1e-9 alone, since the years figure is carried through unrounded."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The longest interval the engine returns in hours is also given in years, derived as the hours over 8760. The decade snap is a relative tolerance for banding and has nothing to do with the interval search, which converges to one part in a trillion. The engine does report a band alongside a PFDavg, so the units of an interval and the band are separate matters.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 8, 'What floor does the OBAGI shutdown valve carry at a proof test coverage of 0.8?', '["0.007905600000", "0.011847600000, which is the floor at a coverage of 0.7", "0.003963600000, which is the floor at a coverage of 0.9", "0.000021600000, which is the floor at a perfect test"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The printed floor at a coverage of 0.8 is 0.007905600000. 0.011847600000, 0.003963600000 and 0.000021600000 belong to coverages of 0.7 and 0.9 and to a perfect test, so each is one row away.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 9, 'How many years may the OBAGI valve''s proof test run against a target of 0.02 when its coverage is 0.7?', '["3.835109 years, which is what the same valve supports at a coverage of 0.8 against the same target", "4.520097 years, the answer the same valve gives at a coverage of 0.9", "2.954410 years", "1.479164 years"]'::jsonb, 2, 'At a coverage of 0.7 the valve supports 25880.634921 hours, 2.954410 years. 3.835109 years belongs to a coverage of 0.8 and 4.520097 years to 0.9. 1.479164 years is the IDU valves against a target of 0.002, a different subsystem searched against a different target.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 10, 'The engine splits every undetected down time when coverage is below one. Which part sees which time?', '["The covered fraction sees the proof test interval and the uncovered fraction sees the restoration time after a test, which is the only time left once the test has missed a failure altogether.", "The covered fraction sees the proof test interval and the uncovered fraction sees the lifetime.", "The covered fraction sees the lifetime and the uncovered fraction sees the interval.", "Both fractions see the interval, with the uncovered one weighted by the lifetime."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The split charges the covered part against the interval and the uncovered part against the lifetime, each with the restoration time after a test added. Swapping the two would make a better test worse. Charging both against the interval is the treatment the engine explicitly does not take, and it is why the floor exists at all.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 11, 'Two things move the floor of a partially tested subsystem. What are they?', '["A shorter proof test interval or a longer lifetime, since both of them change how long the uncovered failures are left sitting in the equipment before it is restored.", "A shorter test interval.", "A longer restoration time after a test or a larger beta factor.", "A better test or a shorter lifetime."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Only a better test or a shorter lifetime moves the floor, because the floor carries no proof test interval in it at all. More frequent testing of the same procedure moves nothing, a target is what the floor is compared against, and a longer restoration time would raise the floor.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 12, 'Why will the engine not accept a proof test coverage below one on its own?', '["Because the uncovered failures stay until the item is restored as new, so without a lifetime there is nothing to average that waiting over.", "Because a coverage below one is outside the range the field allows.", "Because a coverage below one puts the answer outside the rare event range.", "Because the band table has no row for a partially tested subsystem."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s refusal names `lifetimeHours` and gives the reason in those words: the uncovered failures stay until the item is restored as new. A coverage below one is perfectly valid, the rare event range is about the failure rate times the interval, and the band table takes a PFDavg however it was produced.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 13, 'A coverage of 0.9 and a lifetime of 87600 hours meet a target at the lifetime itself. Why is returning the lifetime the honest answer?', '["Because the bisection cannot evaluate the form at an interval longer than the lifetime, so the search has to stop at the last interval it could actually evaluate for the subsystem.", "Because the engine refuses a lifetime shorter than the proof test interval, so an interval past the lifetime is a combination it declines to evaluate.", "Because the target stops constraining the interval once the lifetime is reached.", "Because the lifetime is the point at which the coverage stops applying."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Returning an interval past the lifetime would return a combination the same engine declines, so capping keeps the two halves consistent. The form can be evaluated anywhere, the coverage applies throughout, and the observation that the target no longer constrains the answer is what the state word reports rather than the reason the cap is right.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 14, 'Take the OBAGI valve from a perfect proof test down to a coverage of 0.7, against the same target of 0.02. What happens to its floor and to its longest interval?', '["The floor stays at 0.000021600000, since the restoration time after a test sets it, and the longest interval shortens from 44396.444444 to 25880.634921 hours.", "The floor rises from 0.000021600000 to 0.011847600000 and the longest interval stays at 44396.444444 hours, since the interval answers to the covered share alone.", "The floor rises from 0.000021600000 to 0.011847600000 and the longest interval shortens from 44396.444444 to 25880.634921 hours.", "Both stay where they were."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The coverage table prints a floor of 0.000021600000 and 44396.444444 hours at a perfect test, and 0.011847600000 and 25880.634921 hours at a coverage of 0.7. The floor carries the uncovered share at half the lifetime, so it rises as the coverage falls, and a higher floor leaves less of the target for the interval to use, so the interval shortens too.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 15, 'One golden 2oo2 case reads 0.017520000000 from the engine and 0.017317146501 on the time dependent route. What departure is printed against that pair?', '["6.43 percent, which is the figure printed for a 2oo2 at a beta factor of 0.1", "2.209e-2, which is the figure printed against a 2oo3 with no common cause", "1.512e-1, which is the largest departure anywhere in the table", "1.171e-2"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The printed departure for that pair is 1.171e-2. The 6.43 percent figure compares the Annex B 2oo2 with an exact average at a beta factor of 0.1 and is a separate measurement. 2.209e-2 belongs to a 2oo3 with no common cause, and 1.512e-1 to the long interval single channel.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 16, 'The golden''s long interval single channel reads 0.219000000000 from the engine. What does the time dependent route give, and what does the gap show?', '["0.190241513373, with the engine 15.12 percent above it, the widest gap in the set.", "0.190241513373, and the gap is 1.512e-1, which is the smallest in the set.", "0.017317146501, and the gap is 1.171e-2.", "0.219000000000, since the two routes agree once the interval is long enough."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The time dependent value is 0.190241513373 and the departure is the largest printed anywhere in the set. 0.017317146501 belongs to the 2oo2 case. The two routes never agree exactly, and the gap widens rather than closing as the rare event assumption is strained.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 17, 'The 6.43 percent measured on a 2oo2 at a beta factor of 0.1 carries more than the missing common cause term. What else is in it?', '["The equivalent down time convention and the coverage cross term as well, which is why the figure is larger than any other departure printed anywhere in the set of golden cases.", "The linearisation, so the figure is the total conservatism of that case, of which the missing term is only one part.", "Nothing else, since a 2oo2 has only one departure from the exact average.", "The decade snap, which moves the figure by one part in a billion."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The measured figure carries the linearisation as well as the missing common cause term, so it is the total conservatism of that case. The case carries no coverage, so the cross term is not in it, and it is far from the largest departure printed. The decade snap belongs to banding and moves no PFDavg.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 18, 'What does the linearised single channel form omit, on the validation record''s account?', '["A term of the order of the failure rate times the interval, which is the whole of the rare event correction and is why the form is called linearised in the first place.", "The common cause contribution, which a single channel cannot carry.", "A term of the order of the failure rate times the interval, squared.", "The restoration time of the detected failures, which it charges to the interval."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The exact average subtracts a term of second order in the failure rate times the interval and the published form drops it, which leaves the published form high and higher the larger that product becomes. A single channel genuinely has no common cause term, and the equivalent down time convention charges the detected failures and the restoration times, which is a separate departure.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 19, 'Across the sensitivity sweep, what is the longest interval at which the IDU valves are still banded SIL 3?', '["4380 hours, half a year, where they read 0.000611621000.", "8760 hours, one year, where they read 0.001287026426 and the band has already moved down to SIL 2.", "2190 hours, a quarter of a year, which is the only row of the sweep the valves hold at SIL 3.", "17520 hours."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The band list for the valves reads SIL 3 at 2190 and at 4380 hours and SIL 2 from 8760 hours, so the last SIL 3 row is 4380 hours, at 0.000611621000. At 8760 hours the valves read 0.001287026426, which is SIL 2, and 17520 hours is SIL 2 as well.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 20, 'The engine returns a result together with the rare event warning. What does a verification note do with it?', '["Discards the figure, because a warned figure is not a result the engine stands behind and a note that quotes one is quoting something the engine did not actually say.", "Quotes the time dependent value in its place, since that is the exact average.", "Recomputes the figure at a shorter interval and reports only the recomputed one.", "Carries the warning text alongside the figure, because between the warning and the refusal the engine keeps answering and keeps saying the answer overstates."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The warning is returned WITH a result and the figure is still the number a site is assessed against, so the note carries both. The time dependent value is provenance and never enters a note as an achieved figure, and quietly recomputing at a different interval would report a different subsystem schedule from the one that was asked about.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 21, 'The UNACHIEVABLE test compares a target with the part of the PFDavg that does not depend on T1. Which pieces make up that part?', '["The common cause term and the independent term, since both of them are carried by every redundant subsystem whatever interval is chosen for it.", "The detected failures over the MTTR, the MRT, and the uncovered part under partial coverage.", "The detected failures over the MTTR alone, since the restoration time after a test is charged only when a test has found something.", "Nothing, since every part of the PFDavg grows with T1."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The digest names the three pieces: detected failures over the MTTR, the MRT, and the uncovered part under partial coverage. The independent term and the undetected common cause term both carry T1 and shrink as T1 goes to zero, and the MRT is charged on every undetected failure however often the test runs, which is why a perfect test still leaves the OBAGI valve a floor of 0.000021600000.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 22, 'Read the processor row of the lifetime sweep, printed at 6.12E-07, in its five year column. What stands there?', '["6.12E-07, which is what the row gives at the inferred lifetime of ten years", "5.94E-07, which is what the row gives at a lifetime of eight years", "5.68E-07", "6.55E-07, which is what the row gives at a lifetime of fifteen years"]'::jsonb, 2, 'At a five year lifetime the processor row comes out at 5.68E-07, short of its printed 6.12E-07. That printed figure appears only at ten years; 5.94E-07 is the eight year value and 6.55E-07 the fifteen year one.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 23, 'What does the published coverage table print for the transmitter beta factor, and what value reproduces the row?', '["It prints 10 percent, and the row reproduces at 15 percent, which the heading''s MooN multiplication produces from the printed figure.", "It prints 15 percent, and the row reproduces at 10 percent.", "It prints 10 percent, and the row reproduces at 10 percent once the lifetime is corrected.", "It prints no beta factor at all, so the value had to be inferred from the row."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The table prints 10 percent and reproduces only at 15 percent, which is what the MooN multiplication in its own heading produces. The lifetime is a separate inference that the four row sweep settled at ten years, and the beta factor IS printed, which is exactly what makes this a printing slip in the source.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 24, 'Which printed value does the engine''s 0.000344085754 reproduce?', '["6.76E-04, which is the printed value of the transmitter coverage row", "4.74E-04, which is what the transmitter coverage row gives at the printed beta factor", "1.05E-03, which is the printed value of the valve row", "3.44E-04"]'::jsonb, 3, '0.000344085754 rounds to 3.44E-04, which is the printed value of the transmitter row in the raised beta factor table, at 0.15. 6.76E-04 and 4.74E-04 belong to the coverage row, and 1.05E-03 to the valve.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 25, 'The golden labels the lifetime INFERRED. What does that label buy a reader?', '["It lets a reader treat the value as an input the published source printed, since the label records that the reproduction succeeded with it and the label travels with the value wherever it is used.", "It lets a reader see what the value rests on and recompute the rows at a different lifetime if they disagree with the inference.", "It lets the value be graded, since a labelled inference is a defensible answer.", "It lets the value stand as proof, since four rows reproduced with it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The word travels with the value everywhere it is used, so a reader who disagrees can see exactly what it rests on and recompute. It is not printed in the source, it is never graded because every capstone states its own lifetime, and four rows are strong evidence that still falls short of proof.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 26, 'The dolan-do-1oo2-ptc98 row prints 7.76E-07. In which lifetime column of the sweep does it come out at 7.52E-07?', '["Five years, where the sweep gives that row 7.16E-07", "Twelve years, where the sweep gives that row 8.00E-07", "Eight years.", "Ten years, the lifetime the golden records as INFERRED for all four rows"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The row reads 7.16E-07 at five years, 7.52E-07 at eight, 7.76E-07 at ten, 8.00E-07 at twelve and 8.36E-07 at fifteen. Only the ten year column reproduces the printed 7.76E-07, so 7.52E-07 sits in the eight year column, one of the lifetimes that misses.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 27, 'Which lifetimes did the sweep behind the inference actually try?', '["Five, eight, ten, twelve and fifteen years, and only ten years reproduces all four printed rows.", "Every whole number of years from five to fifteen.", "Ten and twenty years, which bracket the ordinary assumed life.", "Eight, ten and twelve years, the three closest to the ordinary assumed life."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The sweep covers five, eight, ten, twelve and fifteen years. That it tried only five lifetimes is part of why the digest calls the result strong evidence short of proof, so a sweep of every year or of only two or three values would each misstate what was done.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 28, 'Which of these is a fact about the published SOURCE alone?', '["That the restoration time after a proof test is added to every undetected down time while the detected restoration time is charged only on the detected share of the failures.", "That a coverage below one has to arrive with a lifetime.", "That a 2oo2 carries no common cause term.", "That the coverage table prints a beta factor of 10 percent where 15 percent is the value that reproduces its own figures."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The printing slip is a property of the published source, shown here by computing the row both ways. The treatment of the two restoration times, the lifetime requirement and the 2oo2 form are all properties of the engine and of the published equations it implements.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 29, 'Which of these is NOT among the engine''s exported names?', '["maxProofTestInterval", "pfhFromRates", "outcomeFromRequiredRrf", "proofTestSensitivity"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The exported names are, in full, ARCHITECTURES, DECADE_SNAP, HOURS_PER_YEAR, LOPA_OUTCOME, PFD_STATE, SIL_BANDS_LOW_DEMAND, decadeOf, lopaScenario, maxProofTestInterval, outcomeFromRequiredRrf, pfdAvgSif, pfdAvgSubsystem, proofTestSensitivity and silFromPfdAvg. Nothing about a per hour figure is exported, because the engine has no high demand mode.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 30, 'Which three functions make up the determination half of the engine?', '["`lopaScenario`, `outcomeFromRequiredRrf` and `silFromPfdAvg`, which take a row, a required risk reduction factor and an achieved PFDavg.", "`lopaScenario`, `pfdAvgSif` and `pfdAvgSubsystem`.", "`lopaScenario`, `maxProofTestInterval` and `decadeOf`.", "`pfdAvgSubsystem`, `proofTestSensitivity` and `maxProofTestInterval`."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The determination half finds the risk reduction still missing and the band it falls in, which is what those three do. `pfdAvgSif`, `pfdAvgSubsystem`, `proofTestSensitivity` and `maxProofTestInterval` are the verification half, and `decadeOf` is a banding helper.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 31, 'What does `maxProofTestInterval` take as its inputs?', '["A subsystem and a list of intervals in hours, from which it picks the longest that meets the subsystem''s own stated target for the function.", "A subsystem, a target PFDavg and a lifetime, the last of which caps the search.", "A subsystem and a target PFDavg.", "A function and a required PFDavg."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The call takes a subsystem and a `targetPfdAvg`. A list of intervals is what the sensitivity sweep takes. A lifetime is part of the subsystem''s own inputs and is supplied only when the coverage is below one, and the search works on one subsystem at a time.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 32, 'Which five architectures does the engine know?', '["1oo1, 1oo2, 2oo2, 2oo3 and 2oo4, the last being the voted pair of pairs", "1oo1, 1oo2, 1oo3, 2oo3 and 3oo3", "1oo1, 1oo2 and 2oo3 only", "1oo1, 1oo2, 2oo2, 2oo3 and 1oo3"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The exported architecture list is 1oo1, 1oo2, 2oo2, 2oo3 and 1oo3, and an architecture outside it is refused by name. There is no voted pair of pairs and no three out of three in the list, and nothing is reached by combining two entries.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 33, 'Which three states can `silFromPfdAvg` return alongside a band?', '["NOT_SIL_RATED, SIL and BELOW_SIL4_TABLE_FLOOR, the last of which says the figure is off the bottom of the table.", "FOUND, UNACHIEVABLE and CAPPED_AT_LIFETIME.", "SIL1, SIL2 and SIL3, which are the bands a function can claim.", "NO_SIF_REQUIRED, SIL and BEYOND_SIL3_REDESIGN."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The band states are NOT_SIL_RATED, SIL and BELOW_SIL4_TABLE_FLOOR. FOUND, UNACHIEVABLE and CAPPED_AT_LIFETIME are states of the longest interval search, and NO_SIF_REQUIRED and BEYOND_SIL3_REDESIGN are outcomes of a layer of protection analysis row.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 34, 'Why does this course never grade the auditable flag?', '["Its effect on a row is too small to grade at the precision the capstones use.", "Its shared negative control stayed green when the rule was deleted from both sides, so nothing independent checks it and a rule checked for transcription only must not carry a graded answer.", "It is an optional flag, so a capstone cannot rely on an analyst having typed it.", "It is decided by the oracle rather than by the engine, and the oracle is provenance."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The negative control removed the auditable exclusion from both the engine and the oracle at once and the suite stayed green, so the control cannot see the rule. The academy''s standing rule follows from that. The flag''s effect on a row is large rather than small, and the oracle has no separate opinion about it at all.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 35, 'What can the engine not see about a credited protection layer?', '["That the layer''s typed figure lies outside the range a probability may take, which is the one check the engine leaves to the analyst to perform on the row.", "That the same layer has been entered twice under the same name.", "That the layer shares a transmitter or a block with the initiating loop, unless the analyst says so through the flags.", "That a layer flagged independent as the string yes was meant to be credited."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine applies the flags exactly as typed and cannot see what they mean, which is why independence and auditability stay with the people who walked the system. It does check the range of a typed figure, it does refuse the same name twice with case ignored, and a flag that is the string yes is uncredited with its reason given.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 36, 'Stretch every proof test in the teaching function from one year to two. What does the function reach, and why?', '["0.004062218645, which is more than double the one year figure because the redundant subsystems are turning quadratic in the interval.", "0.006819960176, which is the three year figure.", "Exactly twice the one year figure, as for a function linear in T1.", "0.001792971954, which is unchanged because the function is dominated by common cause."]'::jsonb, 0, 'At two years the function achieves 0.004062218645, against 0.001792971954 at one year, so it grows by more than a factor of two. The two redundant subsystems, the 2oo3 transmitters and the 1oo2 valves, carry an independent term that grows with the square of the interval, which is what pushes the sum past double. 0.006819960176 belongs to the three year row, and a figure that merely doubled would describe a function that was linear in the interval throughout.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 37, 'What does the teaching function achieve on a one year test, and what is its band there?', '["0.001792971954, a risk reduction factor of 557.733208, and SIL 3, because the achieved figure sits below the lower bound of the SIL 2 decade on the low demand table.", "0.004062218645 with 246.170895, SIL 2.", "0.006901461880, a risk reduction factor of 146.628422, which is also SIL 2.", "0.001792971954, a risk reduction factor of 557.733208, and SIL 2."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The one year row reads 0.001792971954, 557.733208 and SIL 2. 0.004062218645 with 246.170895 is the two year row. 0.006901461880 is the budget remainder the valves may take, and 146.628422 belongs to the three year row.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 38, 'Across the five stretches of the teaching function, on which intervals does it still meet the tolerable frequency?', '["One year and two years only, because the three year row has already crossed the requirement even though its band has not moved at all from where it started.", "One year, two years, three years and 3.5 years.", "One year, two years and three years.", "Every one of the five."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The verdict column reads true at one, two and three years and false at 3.5 years and at four years. The function is still SIL 2 at 3.5 years, which is exactly why reading the band column alone would have put the crossing in the wrong place.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 39, 'A note reports a proof test interval of 87600.000000 hours. Which state has to be beside it for the reader to know that the target did not decide the number?', '["FOUND, which says the crossing was located and the interval is the crossing point the search converged on", "CAPPED_AT_LIFETIME", "INTERVAL_INDEPENDENT, which says the interval never entered", "UNACHIEVABLE"]'::jsonb, 1, 'CAPPED_AT_LIFETIME says the target is met even at the lifetime, so the lifetime set the number: change the target and the figure will not move. FOUND would mean a crossing was found, INTERVAL_INDEPENDENT would mean the interval never entered, and UNACHIEVABLE would carry no interval at all.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 40, 'What does a verification note record beside each failure rate?', '["Its source, because a rate with nothing beside it can be neither challenged nor defended by a later reader.", "The band the subsystem reached with it.", "The interval at which the rate was measured.", "The oracle''s value for the same quantity."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The note carries each subsystem with its architecture, rates, interval, restoration times, beta factors and coverage, each rate with its source. A band belongs to the subsystem as a whole, a rate is per hour and carries no interval of its own, and the oracle holds no independent rates.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 41, 'The PFDavg of a subsystem never falls as T1 grows. What does that guarantee about a longest interval search?', '["That the search converges in a fixed number of steps whatever subsystem it is given, since a non-decreasing function is bisected the same way every time and to the same depth.", "That the answer is always shorter than the lifetime of the subsystem.", "That every target below one has a finite answer somewhere.", "That the crossing is unique, so the longest interval meeting a target is a single well defined answer."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Because the PFDavg never falls as the interval grows, there is exactly one crossing of the target and the answer is unique. The number of steps is not what the property fixes, a crossing can sit past the lifetime and is then capped, and a target below the floor has no finite answer at all.'),
  ('lopa', 'advanced', 'final', null, 42, 'Under which heading of the note does a warning the engine returned belong?', '["The limits, since a warning is one of the things the engine did not check and therefore belongs beside the architectural constraint and the demand mode in the same heading.", "The caveats, since a warned figure is still a figure the engine computed and stands behind.", "The schedule, since every warning in this engine concerns an interval.", "Nowhere, since a warning carries no number of its own."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The note''s caveats heading holds any warning the engine returned. The limits heading holds what the engine did not check. Two of the three warnings concern a beta factor typed against an architecture that carries none, so not every warning is about an interval, and a warning does arrive with a full result.');
